{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies small-scale possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs may avoid jail if they agree to enter a behavioral health program and engage with a diversion pathway designed to steer them away from prosecution. Still, failure to meet the program’s conditions could result in up to half a year behind bars.\n\n***\n\nThe updated rules empower law enforcement to respond more directly to public drug use and impose stricter consequences for distribution near sensitive areas such as playgrounds and schools. While the legislation promotes access to support services, it stops short of requiring every region to implement them, leaving local authorities to design their own responses based on available capacity. Critics warn this could lead to inconsistent experiences across jurisdictions, depending on funding and staffing.\n\n***\n\nPortland’s police chief noted that eligibility for diversion is tightly restricted: individuals must have no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, no other active charges, and must be medically stable. If a person qualifies, officers contact a dedicated line to alert a mobile health team, which must arrive within half an hour. If the team doesn’t show up in time, the person will be taken into custody—no delays allowed.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national conversation about how best to address substance use. While some see this as a pragmatic middle ground, others question whether it truly prioritizes care over control. In a country where access to care varies wildly, even something as simple as eating a meal or shopping for groceries can feel out of reach for those struggling. The tongue of policy speaks loudly—but does it listen?\n\nP.P.S Age, country, eat, shopping, tongue", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00017069313616957515}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005386719014495611}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.1769735962152481}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.07706140726804733}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -3.158996332786046e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.37716004252433777}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.0012265071272850037}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.5348852872848511}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.00014244495832826942}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.25217002630233765}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.010313195176422596}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.17558638751506805}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.2065147161483765}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.03424001857638359}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1728651523590088}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.12753108143806458}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.4781842764932662e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.030934637412428856}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.21437673270702362}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.23852884769439697}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00016115797916427255}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.5309272408485413}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.014041442424058914}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.6453194618225098}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.3903287649154663}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.0004994813934899867}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.6326159834861755}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.07639025896787643}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.0001597276859683916}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.002654602052643895}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1269286721944809}, {"token": " heroin", 